•
if the error caused the EmailJS account to reach its plan limit, credit Partner against its next invoice for the
cost of one month of the plan upgrade needed to keep the forms running during the billing period in which
the failure happened. Partner sends Ghostwright the EmailJS receipt.
Partner or the Client pays for any later months it chooses to stay on the upgraded plan and is responsible for
downgrading. The correction and the one-month credit are Ghostwright's only obligations for the failure, and
Section 13.4 applies to it. Partner must give notice under Section 8.8.
8.15  Before launch, Ghostwright tests each form under Section 5.5.
8.16  If EmailJS changes or discontinues a feature, quota, or price that affects the forms, Ghostwright will tell
Partner when it learns of the change. Ghostwright will quote any rework needed to move the forms to a different
provider, unless the change was caused by a Ghostwright-Caused Form Failure.
9. White-label and non-solicitation
9.1  Ghostwright delivers each Site and Intake under Partner's brand. It will not add its own name or credit to a
Site or Intake unless Partner agrees in writing and will use Partner's name and email address in everything a
Client sees.
9.2  While this Agreement is in effect, Ghostwright will not solicit, market to, or upsell Partner's Clients, will contact
them only as Section 9.6 allows, and will not do work directly for them on Sites built under this Agreement. When
this Agreement ends, this Section 9.2 stops applying, and Ghostwright may contact any business as an
independent company.
9.3  If a Client contacts Ghostwright directly about a Site built under this Agreement, Ghostwright will refer the
Client back to Partner.
9.4  Section 9.2 does not restrict (a) Ghostwright's general advertising and public website, if not aimed at Partner's
Clients, or (b) businesses that were already clients of Owen Holdeman's separate direct web design business
(Cyberhawk), or of Ghostwright, before Partner sent their Intake.
9.5  Ghostwright may show a Site, with a link to it, and the Client's business name and logo in its portfolio and
marketing. Partner may withdraw this permission, for one Site or for all Sites, at any time by written notice. Partner
confirms it has the Client's permission to give it. Ghostwright will not publish a Client's non-public information in its
portfolio or marketing.
9.6  Communication options. Partner chooses how Ghostwright communicates with Clients, for each Client or
for all Clients, and records the choice in Exhibit B or in writing:
•
Option 1, Partner only (the default). Partner is the only point of contact. Ghostwright does not contact
Clients.
•
Option 2, Partner's web team. Partner gives Ghostwright an email address on Partner's own domain (for
example, developer@partnerdomain.com). Ghostwright uses it to work directly with the Client about
delivery matters only (intake questions, feedback, revisions, DNS help, and content edits), as part of
Partner's team and under Partner's name. Ghostwright's own name does not appear in these messages.
9.7  In every option: (a) Ghostwright invoices only Partner, and Partner alone bills the Client; (b) Ghostwright
never solicits, markets to, or upsells Clients, and does not discuss Partner's pricing, margins, or this Agreement
with them; (c) anything that changes the scope or the price, such as extra revision rounds, new pages, features,
or redesigns, is quoted to and approved by Partner, not by the Client; (d) the timelines in Sections 3, 4, and 8 do
not change; and (e) Partner stays responsible for the Client relationship and for having the Client's agreement to
be contacted directly.
9.8  Under Option 2, Partner will give Ghostwright a shared mailbox or delegated access that Partner can see and
revoke at any time, and will not require Ghostwright to hold Partner's own login credentials. Ghostwright will use
the address only for delivery matters and will not use its own name in messages to Clients. Partner is responsible
Partner Service Agreement, Version 1.2

