for how it describes the arrangement to its Clients, and Ghostwright will not make false statements about its role.
9.9  Partner may change the option for a Client, or for all Clients, by written notice. The change applies to
messages sent after Ghostwright receives it. Ghostwright may decline to start an option it cannot reasonably
support, but will not withhold Option 1. Option 2 carries no extra fee.
10. Ownership, IP, and source code
10.1  Ghostwright owns all rights in each Site's design and source code, and in its reusable tools that are not
specific to a Client, and keeps the source code at all times, except as transferred in a buyout under Section 11.
Partner and the Client use the Site as a managed service and do not own its source files while it is in service.
10.2  While a Site is active and paid for, Ghostwright gives Partner and the Client a non-exclusive,
non-transferable license to use the Site on the Client's domain. Neither may copy or reuse the Site's design or
code for any other website.
10.3  The Client owns its domain and its own content, meaning the text, logos, and photos it supplies. Partner
gives Ghostwright permission to host and display that content and to use it to build and maintain the Site, and
confirms it has the right to give that permission.
10.4  Ghostwright keeps its general know-how, techniques, and skills.
10.5  For an abandoned or cancelled build, Ghostwright keeps its work product and may reuse the design and
code. Before any reuse, Ghostwright will remove the Client's name, logo, photos, reviews, contact details, and
other identifying content.
10.6  Third-party components in a Site stay under their own licenses.
11. Cancellation and buyout
11.1  Partner may cancel any Site at any time by written notice to Ghostwright. There is no cancellation fee.
11.2  A cancellation takes effect on the Cancellation Date, the last day of the calendar month in which Ghostwright
receives the notice. The Site stays online through the Cancellation Date and then goes offline. It is not invoiced for
later months. The current month has already been invoiced and is not refunded.
11.3  On cancellation the Client keeps its domain and its own content, and Ghostwright keeps the source code.
Setup Fees and prepaid months are not refunded. Ghostwright removes its hosting connection for the Site.
Ghostwright gives Partner instructions for the Client to remove the DNS records that point the domain to the Site,
and Partner is responsible for getting the Client to do so.
11.4  Buying the Source Code. Partner may buy a Site's Source Code while the Site is active, or within the
Buyout Window described below after cancelling it, for $3,000 per Site, and may direct that it be assigned to the
Client. The Client may also buy it if Partner consents in writing and the purchase goes through Partner. For an
active Site, Partner may complete the Buyout Steps at any time. For a cancelled Site, Partner must complete the
Buyout Steps within 30 days after the Cancellation Notice Date (the "Buyout Window"). The 30 days start on the
day Ghostwright receives the cancellation notice, not on the Cancellation Date.
11.5  Buyout Steps. Partner arranges a buyout only when all three of these have happened (for a cancelled Site,
by the end of the 30th day of the Buyout Window):
1.Ghostwright has received Partner's written buyout request;
2.Partner has signed the buyout terms that Ghostwright provides; and
3.Ghostwright has received the $3,000 in cleared funds.
Ghostwright will provide the buyout terms promptly after it receives the request. A buyout of a cancelled Site
that is requested but not completed by the end of the Buyout Window is not arranged.
Partner Service Agreement, Version 1.2

Page 8 of 12
11.6  Expiry. When the Buyout Window for a cancelled Site ends, the opportunity to buy that Site's Source Code
under this Section expires. This is true whether or not the live Site has been taken down. A Site that stays online
does not extend the Buyout Window. Ghostwright may agree to an extension only in writing.
11.7  Live Site. The Site stays online only through the Cancellation Date, regardless of the Buyout Window. If the
cancellation notice arrives late in a month, the Site may go offline before the Buyout Window ends. A buyout of a
cancelled Site does not keep it online, and the buyer is responsible for hosting. A buyout of an active Site does not
cancel it. The buyer owns the Source Code as described in Section 11.8. Ghostwright keeps hosting and
maintaining the Site, and the Monthly Fee continues, until Partner cancels under Section 11.1, so Partner may
move the Site to its own hosting whenever it chooses.
11.8  Delivery. After Ghostwright has the signed buyout terms and cleared payment, Ghostwright assigns
ownership of the Site's Source Code, as it stands on the Cancellation Date (or, for an active Site, on the date
Ghostwright prepares the delivery), to Partner (or to the Client, as Partner directs) and delivers it within 10
Business Days. "Source Code" means the Site's files and code that can be packaged (such as in a ZIP) and
moved or deployed elsewhere. It excludes third-party components, the Client's content (which the Client already
owns), credentials, and Ghostwright's hosting account, other accounts, and shared infrastructure. The Source
Code is delivered as is, with no hosting, support, or warranty. Changes Ghostwright makes to an active Site after
the delivery date are not part of the buyout. The buyer may use, modify, host elsewhere, and reuse the Source
Code for any purpose, subject to third-party licenses and rights, and the restriction on copying or reusing the Site's
design and code in Section 10.2 no longer applies to the Source Code the buyer receives. Help requested after a
buyout is new paid work under a separate quote and creates no refund or credit against the buyout price.
11.9  Backup. After a Site is cancelled, Ghostwright keeps a backup, including the Client's content, until 45 days
after the Cancellation Date, or until a completed buyout has been delivered if that is later. Ghostwright keeps the
backup only so a buyout can be completed. Keeping it creates no refund right and does not include any ongoing
service. After that, Ghostwright removes the Client's identifying content from its retained copy under Section 10.5.
11.10  The buyout price is the same whether a Site is new or old, active or cancelled, and is not reduced by fees
Partner has already paid. If Ghostwright ends this Agreement under Section 15.3, Partner may complete the
Buyout Steps for any or all affected Sites during the notice period and for 30 days after it ends.
12. Partner responsibilities
12.1  Partner will send accurate, complete Intakes and Client content on time.
12.2  Materials and instructions. Partner is responsible for the accuracy, authorization, and rights associated
with the materials and instructions that it or its Client supplies, including images, logos, video, copy, and
instructions. Ghostwright is responsible for its own work and its own conduct. If both parties contributed to a
problem, each is responsible for its own share. Ghostwright is responsible if it knowingly uses material outside the
authorization or instructions it received, or otherwise contributes to a problem through its own conduct.
12.3  Authorization confirmation. The Intake requires the person completing it to check a confirmation that the
information is accurate, that they own or have permission to use the logos, photos, text, and other material they
supply, and that they allow it to be used on the Site. The confirmation covers all material supplied in the Intake.
Material the Client or Partner sends afterward is covered by the same confirmation, and sending it renews the
confirmation for that material. Partner confirms that it obtains this authorization from each Client, and that the
Client's submission of the Intake is made with Partner's authority. Ghostwright may rely on the confirmation and
does not have to verify it. Ghostwright receiving an item is not proof that permission exists. Ghostwright may
decline to use, or may remove, an item it reasonably believes is not authorized.
12.4  Partner confirms that it has the Client's permission for Ghostwright to build and host the Site, and that the
content it supplies follows applicable law.
Partner Service Agreement, Version 1.2

Page 9 of 12
12.5  Partner handles all Client communication, including quotes, approvals, support requests, billing, and notices
about suspension or cancellation.
12.6  Partner alone decides how it markets, prices, and bills the Sites. Partner is solely responsible for complying
with the laws and regulations that apply to it, including any rules for its own industry on advertising, referral fees,
or inducements (for example, insurance-department rules for an insurance agency). Ghostwright gives no legal or
insurance advice.
12.7  Licensing, insurance, and credential statements shown on a Site come from the Client or Partner.
Ghostwright does not verify them.
12.8  Partner and the Client are responsible for their own privacy notice and for how they handle the leads and
information that come through the forms, and for any disclosure or consent that tracking on the Site requires.
Ghostwright is responsible for its own handling and configuration.
12.9  Partner will keep the contact details in Exhibit B up to date.
13. Warranties, defects, liability, and indemnity
13.1  Ghostwright will perform the services in a professional and workmanlike manner. Otherwise the services are
provided as is. Ghostwright does not promise any search ranking, traffic, leads, or revenue.
13.2  Defects. Ghostwright will correct, without additional charge, defects in its work that are caused by
Ghostwright's failure to implement the approved specifications or instructions. This may include redoing the
affected sections. It does not include new requests, preference changes, new content, or changes to previously
approved work. Partner will report a defect under Section 8.8. Defects reported within 30 days after Go-Live are
corrected under this section. For a defect found later, Ghostwright corrects it at no charge if it results from
Ghostwright's own error, and otherwise treats the fix as a content edit or quoted work.
13.3  Causation. Responsibility follows what caused the problem. Ghostwright is not responsible for an
independent third-party outage or a Client-side issue that Ghostwright did not cause. Ghostwright is not excused
for its own configuration or implementation error merely because a third-party platform is involved.
13.4  No indirect losses. Neither party is liable to the other for indirect, incidental, or consequential damages,
including lost profits, lost leads, lost revenue, and the value of any job or project a lead might have become.
13.5  Liability cap. Each party's total liability for all claims about a Site is limited to the fees Partner paid or owed
Ghostwright for that Site in the 12 months before the claim. The limit applies to each Site separately. It does not
apply to payment obligations, intentional misconduct, breach of confidentiality, or the indemnities below.
13.6  Partner indemnity. Partner will defend and reimburse Ghostwright for third-party claims arising from Client
content, from materials or instructions Partner or its Client supplied, or from Partner's marketing, pricing, billing, or
regulatory conduct, except to the extent the claim was caused by Ghostwright's own conduct.
13.7  Ghostwright indemnity. Ghostwright will defend and reimburse Partner for third-party claims that
Ghostwright's original code infringes intellectual property rights, and for third-party claims to the extent caused by
Ghostwright's own conduct under Section 12.2. This does not cover Client content, supplied materials, generated
or stock images, or third-party components.
13.8  Claims procedure. The party seeking protection under Section 13.6 or 13.7 will:
•
give the other party prompt written notice of the claim (late notice reduces the other party's duty only to
the extent it was harmed by the delay);
•
let the other party control the defense and settlement, with counsel reasonably acceptable to the
protected party; and
•
cooperate, at the other party's expense.
